Good, I've added the `bloqueios` table creation to `novoBanco`. Now let me also remove the `bloqueios` table creation from `resetarBanco` since it's now created in `novoBanco`. Actually, wait - `resetarBanco` still needs to be able to create/recreate the table. Let me think about this...

Actually, `resetarBanco` does `DELETE FROM ...` followed by `carregarDadosIniciais(banco)`, and then creates the `bloqueios` table with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`. Since the table is now also created in `novoBanco`, the `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` in `resetarBanco` would be a no-op (the table already exists). But the `DELETE FROM bloqueios` is not in the `banco.exec` line - let me check...

Looking at `resetarBanco`:
```javascript
export function resetarBanco(banco) {
  banco.exec('DELETE FROM certificados; DELETE FROM presencas; DELETE FROM inscricoes; DELETE FROM encontros; DELETE FROM atividades; DELETE FROM salas; DELETE FROM usuarios;');
  carregarDadosIniciais(banco);
  // Ensure bloqueios table exists
  banco.prepare('CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bloqueios (participanteId TEXT PRIMARY KEY, nome TEXT, atividades TEXT, bloqueadoDesde TEXT)').run();
  banco.prepare('CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_bloqueios_participante ON bloqueios (participanteId)').run();
}
```

The `banco.exec` line doesn't include `DELETE FROM bloqueios`. So the `bloqueios` table's data is not deleted by the `banco.exec` line. But the `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` would be a no-op if the table already exists (which it does, since I added it to `novoBanco`).

And the `banco.prepare('CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_bloqueios_participante ON bloqueios (participanteId)').run()` would also be a no-op if the index already exists.

Actually, wait. There might be an issue. If the `bloqueios` table is created in `novoBanco` with the schema `participanteId TEXT PRIMARY KEY, nome TEXT, atividades TEXT, bloqueadoDesde TEXT`, and then `resetarBanco` tries to create a unique index on `participanteId`, the index might already exist (since the table already has `participanteId TEXT PRIMARY KEY`, which implicitly creates a unique index). So `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_bloqueios_participante ON bloqueios (participanteId)` might fail because the index already exists (the primary key constraint already creates a unique index).

Hmm, but `IF NOT EXISTS` should handle this - it would do nothing if the index already exists. Let me check if `better-sqlite3` supports `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`...

Actually, I think `better-sqlite3` does support `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`. So this should be fine.

But wait, there might be a naming conflict. The table has `participanteId TEXT PRIMARY KEY`, which implicitly creates an unnamed unique index. And then `resetarBanco` tries to create `idx_bloqueios_participante ON bloqueios (participanteId)`. If the implicit index from the PRIMARY KEY has a different name, then `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_bloqueios_participante` would create a new index with the specified name. This should be fine.

